.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt AUDIOTEST 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm audiotest
.Nd play a PCM test tone or scale, or record one
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar rate Op Ar seconds Op Ar frequency
.Nm
.Fl s
.Op Ar rate
.Nm
.Fl d
.Op Ar rate bits channels
.Nm
.Fl r | R
.Op Ar rate Op Ar periods
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
plays a square wave as signed 16-bit stereo PCM.
The defaults are 48000 samples per second, two seconds of samples,
and a 440 Hz tone.
.Ar rate
must be 8000 through 384000,
.Ar seconds
one through sixty, and
.Ar frequency
20 through half the input rate.
The active driver may reject a rate or resample the input.
.Pp
The program opens
.Pa /dev/console
explicitly, so it works from an SSH session with a pty.
It requires HDA or Sound Blaster 16; the PC speaker is refused.
It retries short writes and fails after five seconds without queue progress,
or when the monotonic clock cannot be read.
After queuing the samples it waits two seconds for the queue and DMA buffers,
then stops playback.
Signals INT, TERM and HUP also stop playback.
The audio stream is global; do not run another audio program concurrently.
.Pp
On HDA,
.Pa /proc/audio
records codec capabilities, connection lists, the chosen path and the last
requested and programmed rates with format readbacks.
Read it after the tone finishes or from another SSH session during playback.
A successful run means samples were submitted, not that a physical output
was audible or its clock was measured.
.Pp
With
.Fl s ,
.Nm
measures the queue on HDA instead.
It writes a second of numbered blocks of 10 ms, every sample the block's
number, as fast as the queue takes them, stalls a second, and writes another
second, reading the
.Cm delay-ms
field of
.Pa /proc/audio
after each block.
It prints the largest delay it read and its bound, six periods of the frame
count nearest 23 ms at the programmed rate, which is what the driver queues
at most, and fails when the delay passed the bound.
.Pp
With
.Fl d ,
.Nm
plays a scale through
.Pa /dev/dsp
instead.
It sets the format,
.Ar bits
8 for unsigned 8-bit or 16 for signed 16-bit, then
.Ar channels ,
1 or 2, then
.Ar rate ,
1000 through 192000, with OSS's requests, or with no arguments sets
nothing and plays at OSS's defaults, unsigned 8-bit mono at 8000 Hz.
It prints the values the driver granted and plays the scale in them: five
square-wave notes of 400 ms each, in stereo 262, 294, 330, 349 and 392 Hz
in the first channel and the octave below in the second, and in mono 80,
100, 111, 222 and 444 Hz.
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SYNC
then waits until the device has played them.
.Pp
With
.Fl r ,
.Nm
records through
.Pa /dev/dsp
instead, as signed 16-bit mono at the rate the device has nearest
.Ar rate ,
48000 by default and 8000 through 192000.
It asks for sixteen periods of 2048 bytes, reads
.Ar periods
of them, 50 by default and at most 1000, one blocking read at a time, then
turns input off with
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SETTRIGGER ,
which stops the device.
It prints what it read and what was left in the buffer, the rate granted,
and the
.Cm capture
line's rate, frames and overruns from
.Pa /proc/audio .
It fails unless it read every period, the rate granted is
.Pa /proc/audio Ns 's ,
the frames read and left are the frames the input stream handed over, so
that none was dropped,
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_GETIPTR
counts at least as many recorded, and there was no overrun.
.Fl R
does the same and then prints the samples it read, 64 bytes to a line, each
line
.Ql audiotest: data:
and the bytes in hexadecimal, for a host that reads the console.
.Pp
The metal RAM image, metal disk root, os-real and the test image stage this
program.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
audiotest 32000 2 440
cat /proc/audio
audiotest -s 48000
audiotest -d 48000 16 2
audiotest -d
audiotest -r 48000 100
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr dsp 4 ,
.Xr proc 5
